# TODO - Offline and multiplayer contracts

## Objective

Verify local and self-hosted play without commercial services.

## Acceptance

- Test host/join, local controllers, LAN clients and disconnect/rejoin.
- Bound and validate untrusted messages; default to local-only services.
- Require no central account for offline games.

## Evidence

Partial evidence, 2026-11-24: `cargo validate --no-cache` passes.
The shared `games::network::Session` policy supports local identities,
explicit opt-in LAN admission, bounded messages, reserved disconnect slots,
and same-origin reconnect. Title-specific codecs reject malformed remote
actions before simulation. Synthetic tests reject unknown identities,
excessive payloads, accidental LAN exposure and over-capacity joins.

The native `games-platform::lan` adapter now provides explicit private-address
TCP binding and bounded frames, with whole-transfer deadlines and connection
closure on invalid or incomplete wire data. Six loopback tests verify
bidirectional framing, opt-in, size and timeout handling, and composition
with session decoding and reconnect using test-controlled identities.
Fresh validation passed in run 1795573514614423045-2067685.

The native adapter now also supports mutually authenticated TLS 1.3 with
explicit local trust roots, server-name verification, leaf certificate
fingerprints and no session resumption. Raw TCP frames are test-only;
production code exposes only the secure adapter. Loopback tests verify
both certificate identities, secure frame boundaries, disconnect/rejoin,
rejection of a wrong host name and an untrusted client, and a bounded
handshake when an unauthenticated socket stops responding.

Fresh validation passed in run 1795576645026554056-2660129.
The tests provision temporary certificates; a real host must enrol trusted
fingerprints and bind them to player slots before admitting any messages.
No real multi-device LAN play or credential enrolment workflow is verified.

The host adapter now composes the shared roster and TLS stream ownership.
A locally configured, unique fingerprint-to-player mapping is bounded by
session capacity. A CA-trusted but unenrolled peer cannot join; neither a
duplicate connection nor a local controller can claim an enrolled identity.
Transport loss reserves the slot, and only the same approved certificate
can rejoin it. The host rejects mismatched frame limits and outbound
connections, applies the title-specific schema before returning a message,
and supports local controllers without any socket or account service.

Physical multi-device LAN play, real credential provisioning and integration
into a playable host remain open. The synchronous host adapter is intended
for an I/O worker; integration with the playable host is still needed.

Fresh validation passed in run 1795576800190768628-2685436 (78 tests).

The listener now limits outstanding unauthenticated sockets to the configured
player count. The permit is released on drop or handshake completion/failure.
A handshake uses the original accepted-socket deadline even after queueing.
Tests cover saturation, capacity recovery and rejection after queue expiry.
Fresh validation passed in run 1795576898196627474-2708567 (80 tests).

The client additionally requires a locally approved host leaf SHA-256
fingerprint during TLS authentication. Standard WebPKI certificate-chain,
name, validity and handshake-signature verification still apply. A different
leaf signed by the same trusted CA is rejected, as is a pinned leaf without
a trusted chain. Certificate rotation requires explicit local re-enrolment;
no production credential provisioning interface is claimed yet.
Fresh validation passed in run 1795577894972235875-2911264 (109 tests).

Host-side enrollment revocation drops active channels and releases both
connected and reserved player identities. Revoked certificates remain
ineligible for this host's application session even when a TLS handshake
can still authenticate their chain. Loopback tests cover active, disconnected,
never-connected and already-revoked identities. Fresh validation passed in
run 1795578187013800242-2949531 (127 workspace tests).

The shared input router now supports exclusive mappings for each attached
controller. Synthetic tests route four simultaneous controllers to distinct
player actions, reject fallback for unbound buttons on assigned devices,
and release old actions on reassignment or detach without cancelling other
held inputs. Window focus loss releases keyboard and touch holds while
preserving controller input. Native device integration remains pending.

The GNU/Linux SDL adapter opens a bounded number of mapped game
controllers and reports initial attachments before the event loop.
Discovery indices are not treated as persistent controller identities;
only admitted instance IDs produce button or removal transitions.
Physical keyboard scancodes, repeat suppression, focus loss and window
close handling are routed independently from title action mapping.
Synthetic tests verify the shared router composition and instance IDs.
An explicitly enabled native smoke in run
1795583972574077445-3709665 opened the input subsystem and delivered a
queued SDL key through its real event pump; unknown controller events
were ignored. Physical controller hot-plug, analogue axes, remapping and
touch-device integration remain unverified.

The local credential importer now accepts one bounded root and directly
signed leaf, consumes a bounded private key through Rustls, and checks
key consistency, certificate validity, trust, role and host name before
admission. Public identity exchange exposes only the root DER and leaf
fingerprint. TLS configurations reuse the certified signing key, while
the host roster independently enforces approved fingerprints and slots.
Loopback tests cover independently issued identities, local players,
approved remote actions, unapproved roots, malformed or mismatched
credentials, expired leaves, duplicate approvals and disabled LAN.
Credential generation and storage remain external; promoting the test
certificate generator to production would introduce incompatible
transitive dependency versions under the workspace's existing lint policy.
An integrated provisioning UI and physical-device LAN remain unverified.

The native host now offers a caller-driven I/O worker with a bounded
request and completion queue, nonblocking submission and result polling,
and correlated operation identifiers. Full or oversized requests return
their original command; shutdown cancels queued work and joins the
in-flight operation. Tests exercise the offline roster, mutual TLS
loopback authentication, schema rejection, encrypted responses, identity
rejoin and deterministic request-queue saturation. Fresh validation
passed in run 1795584885193639323-3848985. The worker serialises transfers:
idle receive requests still use the transport deadline and disconnect,
so callers must submit protocol-expected receives. Independent peer
scheduling and physical LAN play remain unverified.

The caller-driven host worker now also supports an incremental nonblocking
receive operation. An idle authenticated peer does not consume a transfer
timeout or prevent unrelated queued host commands. Incomplete plaintext
headers and payloads retain one deadline across polls; an oversized header,
EOF or expired partial frame closes the connection. A blocking receive can
resume the same partial frame without extending its deadline. Empty frames
are distinct from pending polls. Loopback tests verify partial transfers,
idle polling, schema rejection without losing a subsequent valid frame,
transport-loss slot reservation, and same-certificate rejoin. Fresh
validation passed in run 1795586058926200097-4046029.

Polling is explicitly caller-driven; authentication and outbound sends
still use serialised bounded operations. Physical LAN and an integrated
playable host remain open.

The host worker now advances accepted TLS handshakes incrementally.
A pending completion returns the same socket and permit to the caller
for resubmission; quiet or incomplete negotiation cannot delay unrelated
host commands when this polling path is selected. Authentication retains
the original accepted-socket deadline, verifies the same TLS certificate
and protocol policy, and only then invokes the existing enrolled roster
admission. Failure or cancellation releases the listener permit.

Loopback tests exercise local admission and a second approved TLS client
while a first silent socket remains pending, expiry without renewed
deadlines, and rejection of an untrusted certificate with permit recovery.
The tests passed in both default and all-feature suites. Incremental
outbound sends, automatic scheduling, credential provisioning and physical
LAN play remain open.

The native transport also supports one bounded queued outbound frame per
authenticated channel. Queue admission is distinct from wire completion;
the shared framing cursor handles partial header and payload writes for
both blocking and polled transfers. Polling advances plaintext and flushes
pending ciphertext without waiting for a receiver. Another send cannot
replace or interleave the outstanding frame. Expiry closes the transport
and reserves the existing identity; receiving cannot renew a send deadline.

A deterministic scripted stream exercises short writes, backpressure,
exact frame bytes (including an empty frame), flush completion, queue
rejection and expiry. A TLS loopback worker test verifies a queued send,
busy rejection without disconnecting, unrelated local admission, encrypted
delivery and subsequent idle state. Fresh validation passed in run
1795588148767594009-55007. Caller-driven scheduling remains explicit;
physical LAN, integrated game hosting and production enrolment remain open.

The shared controller router now accepts signed axis events with explicit
per-direction actions and validated press/release magnitudes. Separate
physical axis and button sources aggregate into the existing logical
holds. Hysteresis avoids threshold chatter; reversing an axis bound to
the same action does not emit a spurious release. Exclusive per-device
axis and button mappings release previous holds and do not fall back
to shared mappings. The existing button-only mapping disables axes.

SDL forwards unfiltered signed axis values only for admitted controller
instance IDs. Synthetic tests cover exact thresholds, signed extrema,
direct reversal, independent controllers, overlapping buttons, invalid
thresholds, reassignment and reconnect. This is digital action mapping
from an analog axis, not proportional analog movement. Physical
controller calibration and hot-plug testing remain open.
The opt-in native accelerated smoke also rejected an unknown controller's
axis event. Fresh validation passed with the smoke enabled in run
1795589083936501060-186109.

The shared touch router now accepts device-scoped finger contacts through
an explicit, ordered normalized zone layout. A configured contact limit
bounds admitted fingers; unknown motion and release events are ignored.
Moving between zones changes the existing aggregated logical actions,
and two fingers on one action do not release it until both leave.
Focus loss clears contacts while preserving controller holds.

The SDL adapter offers explicit single-window touch opt-in, with checked
normalized coordinates and position-independent releases. Invalid motion
releases the old action rather than leaving it held. Tests cover zone
priority, contact limits, overlapping actions, focus loss and native event
conversion. Physical touch hardware, title layout integration and Android
and browser adapters remain unverified.
Fresh validation passed in run 1795589553236816708-281632.

Native discovery now re-enumerates available SDL controllers after the
host drains a removal event, preserving the admitted instance identities
and respecting the existing capacity. The title's synthetic integration
test verifies independent movement for four controllers, detach releases,
vacant-slot assignment and rejection of the detached instance's input.
Fresh validation with the native renderer smoke enabled passed in run
1795594686600422366-1677369; repeated discovery did not report duplicate
attachments. Physical controller replacement remains unverified.

The framed transport uses the earliest outstanding inbound or outbound
deadline for subsequent socket work in either direction. An expired
partial receive now closes the connection even when the caller polls
only outbound progress or attempts a blocking send. A loopback test
covers idle outbound polling, queued outbound polling and blocking
transmission with a previously admitted partial header.

Outbound TCP establishment and mutual TLS negotiation now share a
single absolute deadline, rather than resetting the timeout after TCP
connect. Final certificate admission also checks the remaining time.
Loopback regressions verify that expired outbound attempts do not
open sockets, successful TCP establishment preserves its deadline,
and both TLS roles reject publication after negotiation expiry.

The worker's loopback regression composes two independently enrolled TLS
clients with a local controller. One client's incomplete frame does not
prevent the other from delivering a validated action; the first can then
complete its own frame. Disconnect and same-certificate rejoin preserve
that player's identity without interrupting the other client. This is
caller-driven scheduling evidence, not physical LAN or gameplay parity.

The Simpsons cabinet now provides a strict version-one, three-byte remote
control codec. Frames contain one ordinary station control and its binary
transition; they cannot specify a player, tick or host-only Pause command.
A transport-neutral hosted cabinet consumes the host's verified lifecycle
events and decoded messages through the same ordered fixed-step interface
as local play. Explicit seat reservations prevent local devices from
controlling a remote seat. Only local seats can start the world or control
host menus; disconnect and host suspension release remote holds before
movement resumes. At least one local seat is required.

Synthetic tests cover every possible control byte, invalid state and
version, exact lengths, roster identity and size rejection, two independent
remote seats alongside a local station, seat isolation, disconnect/rejoin
and host-only menu transitions. This adapter does not open a listener,
schedule the I/O worker, provision credentials or replicate game state.

The caller-driven host input pump now binds an explicitly opted-in private
endpoint, reserves distinct local and remote identities and automatically
schedules bounded handshake and receive work on the existing I/O worker.
Ready and in-flight work is correlated and rotates through peers; a quiet
pending socket or incomplete authenticated frame cannot block other input.
It exposes only authenticated admission, schema-checked input and transport
loss, and does not drop lifecycle events on behalf of its caller.

The loopback tests exercise two approved peers and one local player, a
silent pending socket, independent partial-frame delivery, malformed
message rejection and continued valid input, disconnect and verified
rejoin. Disabled LAN, empty enrolment and excess reserved capacity fail
before opening a listener. Fresh validation passed in joint run
1795602334492857230-2957088. Native title composition, outbound state,
credential provisioning and physical LAN play remain open.

The native Simpsons LAN bridge now composes the authenticated host pump,
title decoder, reserved cabinet and shared bounded live input queue.
Network work is limited per presentation poll; every verified event is
assigned the next unexecuted simulation tick. If the queue is full, one
rejected event is retained and retried before any further network polling,
rather than losing a disconnect or release.

An all-feature test uses independently issued temporary TLS credentials
and real loopback sockets to join and move the approved remote station.
It deliberately fills a one-action simulation queue before connection
loss and verifies that the remote hold stops after retry while the local
station keeps moving. The same certificate then reconnects and moves the
same station in the opposite direction. The full fresh gate passed in
1795602549609489814-2994574. Native window launch and a remote controller
application are still required for interactive play.

The interactive native Simpsons window now accepts an explicit private
host catalogue. Bounded DER certificate reads and owner-only private
key admission precede mutual TLS listener creation. Each approved
leaf fingerprint maps to one reserved input seat. Keyboard mappings,
controller assignment and join prompts respect reservations; touch
cannot select a remote seat. The existing interactive simulation polls
the bridge before fixed updates, retaining normal rendering, audio,
menus and display preferences. Offline play remains the default; hidden
smoke rejects network hosting. Native remote controller and physical
multi-device acceptance are still open.

A standalone native controller now uses an explicit catalogue and the
same protected local credential admission with an independently approved
host leaf and root. It does not load canonical game bundles. The small
static window uses the existing keyboard and gamepad mappings, omits
Pause from the wire, waits for input between nonblocking polls, and
redraws only in response to native events. Its finite outgoing queue
retains transition order; overflow or transport failure exits rather
than dropping a release. The host remains the sole world display.

The real TLS queue regression delivered Start, Right press and Right
release through the shared fixed-tick bridge and verified that movement
stopped. It also rejected an extra edge at capacity. Explicit controller
catalogue parsing and an opt-in native controller panel readback passed.
Fresh validation with Xvfb, software GL and dummy audio passed in run
1795603183924768559-3103778. Physical multi-device input, remote world
presentation, partition detection and credential issuance remain open.

The shared input pump supports an optional authenticated application
idle lease. Only a decoded message renews it; pending or malformed input
does not. The Simpsons controller sends an idle heartbeat every 500 ms,
without overtaking controls. Its host expires a silent peer after three
seconds and delivers the ordinary disconnect event to the next eligible
fixed tick. Heartbeats never enter the simulation input queue. A real
TLS regression with a shorter lease kept an active heartbeat connection
alive beyond expiry, stopped heartbeats without closing the client socket,
verified held movement stopped, and rejoined with the same certificate.
Fresh joint validation passed in run 1795603408838445579-3134552.
Physical network interruption and multi-device latency remain unverified;
host polling and queue backpressure can delay processing the expiry.

A two-process native loopback diagnostic exercised the release binary
at 29d457d using a private X11 display, SDL software OpenGL, and the
locally imported world and pose bundles. Independently issued P-256
roots and role-specific leaves were generated with OpenSSL, then
admitted through the existing bounded DER catalogues. Native keyboard
events started the host's local player and joined the reserved second
seat from the controller window. Right and Left changed the host's
actor pixels; consecutive 960x672 host captures were pixel-identical
after release and after terminating the controller with a held key.
A fresh controller process using the same approved credentials moved
the remote station again. No game assets were loaded by the controller.
This demonstrates the actual native event and transport path on one
machine, not physical multi-device play or a remote world renderer.

A second native diagnostic executed the documented manual credential
recipe on both endpoints. The controller rejected a different host
leaf signed by the same trusted root and separately rejected a local
private key with group/other read permissions. Restoring the exact
approved leaf and owner-only mode admitted the controller. These tests
used disposable local identities; all generated private key files
were removed after the runs.
